I’d position **VibeTour** around “ambient travel while coding” first, and only secondarily around the vehicle/IDE transformation.

# VibeTour
## Product Requirements Document

**Working name:** VibeTour  
**Category:** Immersive developer environment / ambient coding / spatial IDE interface  
**Initial platform:** VS Code-compatible extension + companion display  
**Future platforms:** Cursor, Windsurf, standalone desktop environment, Claude Code/Codex integrations  
**Core promise:** **See the world while you build.**

---

# 1. Product Summary

VibeTour transforms a coding session into an immersive journey through real and imagined destinations.

While the user codes, reviews AI-generated work, runs tests, uses tools, or supervises coding agents, they appear to travel through a destination of their choosing: coastal California, Tokyo at night, the Swiss Alps, Tuscany, Iceland, Route 66, Paris, the Scottish Highlands, or other environments.

The user experiences the journey from inside a vehicle or cockpit.

The cockpit is not merely decorative.

Its dashboard, navigation screen, mirrors, instruments, indicators, radio, center console, windshield, and other surfaces become graphical representations of development information such as:

- files
- Git status
- tests
- errors
- AI agents
- context usage
- terminal activity
- current task
- project progress
- documentation
- chat

VibeTour should feel less like a game layered onto an IDE and more like:

> **A working vacation for people who spend all day inside code editors.**

The user gets the psychological experience of movement, scenery, novelty, and destination while still retaining fast access to a professional coding environment.

---

# 2. Product Thesis

Modern developers increasingly spend long stretches looking at nearly identical windows:

- editor
- terminal
- AI chat
- file tree
- browser
- Git
- test output

The actual work may be exciting, but the visual environment rarely changes.

At the same time, AI-assisted coding is shifting developers from constant typing toward a mix of:

- instructing
- watching
- reviewing
- waiting
- approving
- debugging
- supervising agents

This creates an opportunity for the visual workspace itself to become more experiential.

VibeTour asks:

> What if spending four hours coding could also feel like spending four hours traveling through Japan?

Instead of measuring productivity primarily through keystrokes, VibeTour interprets meaningful development activity and translates it into motion through an environment.

The destination becomes an ambient representation of a work session.

---

# 3. Positioning

VibeTour should **not** primarily market itself as:

“Gamified coding.”

“Drive a car while you program.”

“A racing game for developers.”

Those descriptions make the product sound disposable.

Preferred positioning:

> **See the world while you code.**

> **Turn coding sessions into journeys.**

> **Your IDE could use a better view.**

> **Work somewhere new every day.**

> **Code in California today. Tuscany tomorrow. Tokyo tomorrow night.**

The transportation metaphor supports the experience but should not overwhelm it.

The user is not racing.

They are traveling.

---

# 4. Core Product Principles

## 4.1 Travel first

The scenery and sensation of moving through a place are core value, not wallpaper.

Destinations should feel distinctive.

Tokyo should not simply be “generic road + Japanese signs.”

Routes should eventually include recognizable architectural, geographic, environmental, and atmospheric characteristics.

---

## 4.2 Coding remains primary

VibeTour must never make ordinary development substantially slower.

Every immersive interaction must have a fast conventional equivalent.

A user can enjoy opening the “glovebox” to retrieve documentation, but they should never be forced to watch a glovebox animation when a keyboard shortcut would be faster.

---

## 4.3 Information becomes instruments

Instead of permanently showing dozens of panels, VibeTour collapses information into meaningful visual indicators.

Details expand only when requested.

This allows a quieter workspace without hiding important state.

---

## 4.4 Movement represents useful activity

Vehicle movement should not equal keystrokes.

Useful activity can include:

- user coding
- AI coding
- reviewing
- navigating
- running commands
- searching
- tests
- builds
- agent activity

Thinking is part of programming and must not be punished.

---

## 4.5 Arrival has meaning

Reaching a destination should correspond to meaningful progress.

It should not simply mean “you typed for 45 minutes.”

A journey may represent:

- one coding session
- one issue
- one feature
- one milestone
- one project phase

---

# 5. Primary Users

## AI-assisted developer

Uses Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Windsurf or similar tools.

Often spends significant time supervising AI rather than manually typing every line.

VibeTour provides an ambient representation of what the human and agents are doing.

---

## Vibe coder / indie builder

Builds apps for several hours at a time.

Values enjoyable environments, customization and visual novelty.

This may become VibeTour's strongest early adopter group.

---

## Remote developer

Works from the same office or room every day.

VibeTour provides psychological variety without requiring additional physical travel.

---

## Developer streamer/content creator

Uses VibeTour because it creates a visually distinctive coding environment for:

- YouTube
- Twitch
- TikTok
- X
- demos
- build-in-public content

This could become an important organic distribution channel.

---

# 6. Core Experience

The user opens VibeTour.

They see:

**WHERE DO YOU WANT TO WORK TODAY?**

Example destinations:

San Francisco → Big Sur  
Tokyo at Night  
Swiss Alps  
Tuscany  
Scottish Highlands  
Iceland Ring Road  
Pacific Northwest  
French Riviera

The user chooses:

**Tokyo at Night**

