# 7. Primary Display Modes

## Mode A — Tour Mode

The most immersive experience.

The exterior environment occupies most of the screen.

Minimal development information remains visible.

Ideal for:

- agent work
- thinking
- reviewing
- ambient sessions
- second monitor

---

## Mode B — Dashboard Mode

The cockpit remains visible, but development instruments become more prominent.

Useful during active work.

Example visible state:

- active file
- task
- Git changes
- errors
- agent status
- test status
- context
- project progress

---

## Mode C — Work Mode

The windshield and dashboard transform into a high-density coding workspace.

The outside environment recedes or disappears.

The user gets something close to a conventional IDE arrangement.

Example:

Explorer | Editor | AI

Terminal along bottom.

Tour continues invisibly in the background.

Returning to Tour Mode restores the exterior environment.

---

## Mode D — Companion Mode

VibeTour runs on a second display while the user keeps their existing IDE untouched.

This may be extremely important for V1 adoption.

The companion display shows:

- route
- scenery
- activity
- agents
- tests
- context
- task state
- Git state
- journey progress

Companion Mode lets users experience VibeTour without changing their established coding workflow.

