# 24. Accessibility

VibeTour must include:

- Reduced Motion
- Static Scenery
- Low GPU Mode
- Disable Weather Effects
- Disable Camera Movement
- scalable UI
- keyboard navigation
- high-contrast instruments
- screen-reader-accessible equivalent controls
- independent sound controls

Users prone to motion sickness must be able to enjoy VibeTour without simulated vehicle motion.

Static Mode could show a rotating sequence of scenic working locations instead.

---

# 25. Performance Requirements

VibeTour must not materially interfere with the developer's actual work.

Targets:

- renderer isolated from critical IDE processes
- configurable FPS
- 30 FPS low-power option
- 60 FPS premium option
- automatic GPU throttling
- pause rendering when hidden
- minimal CPU usage when Work Mode occupies the screen
- low memory overhead
- graceful fallback for integrated graphics

Laptop battery impact should be visible and controllable.

---

# 26. Privacy

VibeTour should not require source code to leave the user's machine merely to animate the environment.

The activity engine should preferably consume metadata such as:

- editing active
- tests running
- agent active
- diagnostics count

rather than source contents.

Any cloud features requiring code access must be separately disclosed and optional.

